#include <stdio.h>
int main() 
{
    int val1, val2;
    int sum, diff, prod;
    float quotient, average;
    printf("Enter first integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &val1);
    printf("Enter second integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &val2);
    sum = val1 + val2;
    diff = val1 - val2;
    prod = val1 * val2;
    quotient = (float)val1 / val2;  
    average = (sum * 1.0f) / 2;     
    printf("\n--- Calculation Results ---\n");
    printf("Sum        : %d\n", sum);
    printf("Difference : %d\n", diff);
    printf("Product    : %d\n", prod);
    printf("Quotient   : %.2f\n", quotient);
    printf("Average    : %.2f\n", average);
    return 0;
}

printf prints clear instructions and results directly to the screen, while scanf takes whatever integer the user types and safely stores it into val1 and val2 using the address operator (&).
The program also handles type conversion smoothly to ensure the math remains accurate. In C, dividing two whole numbers normally drops any fractional remainder. 
To prevent this, this code uses two effective approaches:
For the quotient: Writing (float)val1 temporarily changes val1 into a decimal number. This forces C to perform standard decimal division rather than throwing away the remainder.
For the average: Multiplying the total sum by 1.0f promotes the numerator into a floating-point value. Dividing that decimal result by 2 ensures the final average retains its precise decimal value.
